<BaseLayout title="The Bay Area, Again and Again — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Seven visits to the San Francisco Bay Area, 2022–2026: a castle winery in Napa, the Highway 1 coast to Capitola and 17-Mile Drive, Twin Peaks at night, the skyline from Treasure Island, a June week in the South Bay and Peanuts statues in Santa Rosa.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2022–2026</p>
  <h1>The Bay Area, Again and Again</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Seven visits in five years: wine country, the city, and the coast below it.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>Seven visits · May 2022 – August 2026</strong><br />
    May 2022: a campus-and-coast loop · May 2023: Memorial Day weekend, Napa and Highway 1 ·
    November 2023: Thanksgiving, Twin Peaks and Treasure Island · November 2025: the end of an Oregon trip ·
    June 2026: a week in the South Bay and a dinner on the Embarcadero ·
    August 2026: the city, then Santa Rosa · August 2026: Santa Cruz and Carmel after Tahoe<br />
    one castle winery, visited twice · the same Capitola street, a year apart · the Embarcadero twice in one summer
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-overview.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-overview.webp'} alt="Map of Northern California from Santa Rosa and Napa Valley down to Carmel, marking stops from seven visits: Santa Rosa, Castello di Amorosa, Napa, the Golden Gate, the Embarcadero, Twin Peaks, Treasure Island, Pacifica, the Palo Alto Baylands, Santa Cruz, Capitola, Bird Rock and Carmel." /></a>
    <figcaption>Seven visits on one map. Each stop is labeled with the visit it belongs to.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Some places you visit once and tick off. The Bay Area is not one of them for us. We keep coming back, and when
    I try to explain why, it comes down to three plain reasons. First, it is close to Napa and the rest of wine
    country, so a weekend in the city can always turn into an afternoon among the vineyards. Second, San Francisco
    simply has a lot to see, more than any one visit can hold. Third, it sits on the way north. Every drive to
    Oregon passes through it, and stopping for a night or two is the natural thing to do.
  </p>
  <p>
    So this page is not one trip. It is seven, lined up in order, from a long loop in May 2022 to a stop on the
    coast at the end of a Tahoe trip in August 2026. Two of them, a Memorial Day weekend and a Thanksgiving drive,
    both in 2023, get the full treatment here, and so do two shorter ones from the summer of 2026: a week in June
    and a weekend in August. The other three have their own posts, so they appear only briefly, with a link. As always, the timeline comes from the camera roll. Where the photos are silent, so am I.
  </p>

  <h2>May 2022: the long loop</h2>
  <p>
    The first visit was the biggest. In May 2022 we drove up from Southern California and spent a week going round
    the Bay: Berkeley, San Francisco, Stanford, the Silicon Valley campuses, then Capitola and Highway 1 on the way
    home. That trip has its own full post, <a href="/misc/roadtrips-2022">Two Road Trips on a Brand-New License</a>, so here is just
    one evening from it. On May 18 we walked the Coastal Trail in the Presidio at about seven in the evening, with
    the Golden Gate Bridge lit from the side and the hills of Marin across the strait.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'golden-gate-2022.webp'} alt="The Golden Gate Bridge's south tower seen from the Coastal Trail, with Marin's hills behind and the bridge road sweeping toward the city." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Golden Gate from the Coastal Trail, May 18, 2022.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sea-cliff-2022.webp'} alt="Green coastal bluffs dropping to the Pacific, with the houses of Sea Cliff and the Richmond District in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Looking the other way, toward Baker Beach and Sea Cliff.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    That one evening already shows why the city keeps pulling us back. Turn one way and you have the most
    photographed bridge in the world. Turn the other, and the same path looks down cliffs covered in ice plant to
    the open Pacific, with whole neighborhoods stacked on the far headland. A week was not enough to see it all,
    which is the most honest reason we came back.
  </p>

  <h2 id="may-2023">Memorial Day weekend, May 2023</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-2023-05.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-2023-05.webp'} alt="Map of Memorial Day weekend 2023: arrival at SFO, a day trip to Castello di Amorosa in Napa Valley, then Golden Gate Park, Pacifica, the San Mateo coast, Capitola and Bird Rock on 17-Mile Drive." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Memorial Day weekend: Napa on Saturday, the whole coast on Sunday.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    A year later we came back for a long weekend, and this time I flew in, from Seattle. I was spending that summer
    on an internship in Redmond, Washington, having flown up from LA for it, so San Francisco was a short hop south.
    The trip is short, three days from
    Friday night to Sunday evening, but it covers a surprising amount of ground. Saturday went to wine country.
    Sunday started in the city and then ran down the coast, a hundred-odd miles of Highway 1, all the way to
    Pebble Beach.
  </p>

  <h3>Arriving at dusk</h3>
  <p>
    The flight came in from the north. At 8:51 pm the phone put the plane over southern Oregon, at cruising altitude, at
    the moment the sun went down. Above the clouds, sunset is a different thing. There is no skyline or horizon of
    hills, just a thin line of orange and red stretched across the whole window, with deep blue above it and a dark
    floor of cloud below. It lasted a few minutes and then faded to a glow.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'oregon-sunset.webp'} alt="View from a plane window of a thin orange and red sunset line above a dark layer of clouds, with a deep blue sky above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset over southern Oregon, from the plane, 8:51 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Forty minutes later we were coming down over the Peninsula. The approach into SFO runs low over the
    southern part of the Bay, and at night it is one of the best views you can get of the region: the
    shoreline drawn in lights, the San Mateo Bridge to the East Bay as a straight bright line, and the
    towns of Menlo Park, Foster City and San Mateo fading into one another. The wingtip in the corner of the frame
    tilted as we turned toward the runway.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'night-approach.webp'} alt="Night view from a plane of the lit-up shoreline of San Francisco Bay, with a long straight line of bridge lights and a wingtip in the corner." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Bay on approach, over Foster City, 9:35 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Castello di Amorosa</h3>
  <p>
    Saturday was for wine country. A little after one in the afternoon we pulled up to <strong>Castello di
    Amorosa</strong>, near Calistoga at the top of Napa Valley. The approach sets the tone before you see anything:
    a lane lined with tall, thin Italian cypresses, with vineyards on both sides and the dry, forested hills of the
    valley beyond. It looks more like Tuscany than California, which is the point.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'castello-cypress.webp'} alt="A road through vineyards lined with tall, narrow Italian cypress trees, with hills in the background." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The cypress-lined lane at Castello di Amorosa.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then the castle itself appears, and it is a real one, at least in the way it was built. Castello di Amorosa is
    a thirteenth-century-style Tuscan castle that Dario Sattui spent well over a decade building, using
    old-fashioned methods and hand-cut stone, and it opened as a winery in 2007. It has towers, crenellated walls, a
    moat with a drawbridge, a great hall and a dungeon, and wine is made and aged inside it. That May afternoon the
    sky was a hard, cloudless blue, and red roses bloomed in front of the stone gatehouse.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'castello-di-amorosa.webp'} alt="A medieval-style stone castle with a tall crenellated tower and battlements under a deep blue sky, with an olive tree at left." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Castello di Amorosa, 1:06 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    We did a tasting, starting around a quarter past one with a glass of rosé, and an hour later we were back in
    the car with a small souvenir. It was a kraft-paper box printed with a drawing of the castle and its
    vineyards, and inside were six chocolates: two glossy red hearts, two crowns and two plain bars. We photographed
    them on St. Helena Highway. It is a very Napa thing to do. The winery sells you the wine, and the
    gift shop makes sure you leave with something to eat on the way.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'castello-box.webp'} alt="A kraft-paper gift box printed with a drawing of a castle among vineyards and the words Castello di Amorosa, Napa Valley." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The souvenir box.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'castello-chocolates.webp'} alt="An open box of six chocolates: two shiny red hearts, two crown-shaped chocolates and two rectangular bars." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hearts and crowns, on St. Helena Highway.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The last photo of the day was taken in St. Helena at a quarter to four, and after that the camera roll goes
    quiet. We drove back to the city that evening, and nothing else from Saturday made it into the phone.
  </p>

  <h3>Down the coast on Sunday</h3>
  <p>
    Sunday began on the west side of San Francisco, and it began gray. That is normal here in late May. The coast
    has its famous summer fog season, and in the Sunset District the sky is often a flat white ceiling until
    midday. We drove through <strong>Golden Gate Park</strong> on Crossover Drive at 11:24, and in front of us one
    of the city's steep, wooded hills rose over rows of pale houses, its top lost in mist.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sunset-district-hill.webp'} alt="A steep hill crowned with trees, covered with pale houses, seen through trees on an overcast morning in western San Francisco." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Leaving Golden Gate Park, 11:24 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Five minutes later we were on 19th Avenue, which is how most people meet the Sunset District. The houses here
    were built side by side in the 1930s and 40s, each with a garage on the ground floor and a bay window above.
    They are all the same shape and almost all different colors: pale blue, cream, mint, yellow, a dark red one at
    the corner. Under a gray sky they look like a row of paint chips.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sunset-row-houses.webp'} alt="A row of narrow attached houses in pastel colors along a wide avenue under an overcast sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>19th Avenue, Sunset District, 11:29 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From there we headed south on <strong>Highway 1</strong>. The road leaves the city at Daly City, climbs past
    the cliffs and drops into <strong>Pacifica</strong>, the first real beach town south of San Francisco. At 12:11
    we were on the highway above the beach: a long curve of sand, white surf rolling in, a green headland with
    houses climbing it, and the fog sitting low over all of it. A speed-limit sign stands in the middle of the
    view, as if to remind you that you are still on a highway.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pacifica.webp'} alt="A wide beach with breaking surf and a green headland with houses, seen from Highway 1 through a guardrail on a foggy day, with a speed limit sign." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Highway 1 above the beach at Pacifica, 12:11 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    South of Half Moon Bay, the coast empties out. For miles the highway runs along low bluffs with farmland on one
    side and the ocean on the other, with almost no towns. Forty minutes after Pacifica we pulled off on a bluff north
    of San Gregorio, most likely just a roadside turnout, nothing with a name. Below us, coastal scrub ran down to the edge, and a beach curved off to a pale cliff in the
    distance, with the surf coming in in long white lines.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'san-mateo-coast.webp'} alt="Green coastal scrub on a bluff above a curving beach and a pale headland, under a gray sky, with a power line on the right." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The San Mateo coast, north of San Gregorio, 12:51 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'san-mateo-surf.webp'} alt="Surf breaking on a gray beach below a bluff covered in coastal shrubs." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same bluff, looking down at the surf.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Capitola</h3>
  <p>
    By a quarter past two we were in <strong>Capitola</strong>, just past Santa Cruz. It is one of the oldest
    seaside resort towns in California, and it has one of the most photographed rows of buildings on the coast:
    the Venetian Court, built in the 1920s. Its little stucco buildings are painted in candy colors, lavender, mint,
    tangerine, bright yellow and pink, and their walls are textured like icing. The rows step down toward the
    beach, and every turn of the path shows a new combination of colors.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'capitola-houses.webp'} alt="Brightly painted stucco buildings in orange, yellow, pink, blue and mint green with textured walls, under a gray sky in Capitola." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The candy-colored buildings of Capitola, 2:13 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Capitola is also where the two Bay Area trips overlap. A year earlier, on the 2022 loop, we had stopped in the
    same spot on the way home. That is how this place works for us: the same corners keep turning up, and each
    time they look a little different. In 2023 they stood under the same flat gray sky as the rest of the
    coast, which, if anything, made the colors louder.
  </p>

  <h3>Bird Rock, 17-Mile Drive</h3>
  <p>
    The last stop was the farthest south. <strong>17-Mile Drive</strong> is a private toll road that loops through
    Pebble Beach and the Del Monte Forest on the Monterey Peninsula, past golf courses, mansions, cypress groves and
    a string of coastal turnouts. Its most famous sight is the Lone Cypress, a single tree on a granite outcrop that
    has stood there for more than two centuries. But the stop that held us longest was <strong>Bird Rock</strong>,
    at five in the afternoon.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bird-rock.webp'} alt="A low rocky island offshore, white with guano and dotted with dark seabirds, in a gray sea under clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bird Rock, 4:58 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Bird Rock is exactly what it sounds like: a low granite island a short way offshore, painted white by
    generations of seabirds. From the turnout it looks like a pale lump in the sea. Zoom in and it turns out to be
    covered with birds, mostly dark cormorants packed shoulder to shoulder along its top, with gulls on the
    edges. Sea lions and harbor seals haul out on the rock too,
    and the water around it was full of floating kelp.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bird-rock-close.webp'} alt="Close view of Bird Rock, its top covered with dark cormorants, its sides streaked white, above gray water with kelp." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Zoomed in: the top of the rock, covered with cormorants.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The shore side was just as busy. A western gull stood on a granite boulder above a small white-sand cove, and on
    a flat outcrop farther along, a dozen more gulls sat facing into the wind, ignoring the surf behind them. Just
    below the turnout, on a patch of sand between the boulders and a piece of driftwood, a few seals lay in a heap,
    almost the same color as the sand.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bird-rock-gull.webp'} alt="A gull standing on a granite boulder above a small cove with white sand and rocks." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A gull above the cove.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bird-rock-seals.webp'} alt="A few seals lying on sand beside large granite boulders and a driftwood log." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Seals resting between the boulders and a log.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The last photo of the weekend was taken at 5:01 pm: gulls on a flat rock, gray sea, gray sky. The camera roll
    stops there and says nothing about the way home. It is a good place to end. We had started the weekend
    watching a sunset over Oregon, and we finished it on the edge of the continent with a few hundred birds for
    company.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bird-rock-gulls.webp'} alt="A group of gulls resting on a flat, streaked rock with gray surf and sea behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Gulls on 17-Mile Drive, 5:01 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="nov-2023">Thanksgiving, November 2023</h2>
  <p>
    Six months later we were back, this time by car. The plan was simple: drive up from Southern California on
    Thanksgiving afternoon and spend the long weekend in and around the city. It turned into a trip of very
    different moments: a sunset on I-5, a second visit to the castle, a night on Twin Peaks, and a blue morning on
    Treasure Island.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-2023-11.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-2023-11.webp'} alt="Map of Thanksgiving weekend 2023: Castello di Amorosa in Napa Valley, Corte Madera in Marin, Twin Peaks at night and Treasure Island the next morning." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Thanksgiving weekend in the Bay Area, after the drive up I-5.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>I-5 on Thanksgiving</h3>
  <p>
    We left on Thanksgiving Day itself, in the middle of the afternoon. By 4:42 pm we were in the Central Valley near Kettleman City, on the long straight stretch of I-5 where the
    road runs between flat, dry fields and the low hills of the Coast Range.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'i5-sunset.webp'} alt="An orange sunset just touching a low line of hills across flat farmland." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset over the Coast Range from I-5, 4:42 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'central-valley.webp'} alt="Flat golden grassland under a hazy pastel sky with transmission towers in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Central Valley, a minute later.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Late November sunsets come early, and this one came exactly as we reached the valley. The sun dropped onto the
    hills to the west and lit the whole sky orange for a few minutes. Then the land turned the color of straw and
    the sky went pale pink and lavender, with power lines marching off toward the horizon. After that it was a long
    drive in the dark.
  </p>

  <h3>Back to the castle</h3>
  <p>
    On Friday, the day after Thanksgiving, we went back to Napa: a tasting at Del Dotto, lunch at Farmstead in
    St. Helena, and then back to <strong>Castello di Amorosa</strong>. That is the clearest sign of how this place works for us: the castle was good enough the
    first time that we chose it again, six months later, in a different season. The only photo from that
    afternoon, at 4:30 pm, was taken a few yards from where we had stood in May, and it isn't one worth publishing.
    The visit itself was.
  </p>
  <p>
    From Napa we drove south through Marin. At seven in the evening we were in <strong>Corte Madera</strong>, at
    the shopping center by the freeway, for a little Black Friday shopping. Then we crossed the Golden Gate Bridge into
    the city and drove up the hill.
  </p>

  <h3>Twin Peaks at night</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Twin Peaks</strong> is the pair of hills near the geographic center of San Francisco, a little over 900
    feet high. From the top, the whole eastern half of the city is laid out at your feet. We got there at 9:39 pm.
    The most striking thing from up there is <strong>Market Street</strong>. It runs dead straight from the foot of
    the hill to the Ferry Building on the bay, and at night it is a ribbon of orange streetlights cutting
    diagonally across the dark grid, aimed straight at downtown.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'twin-peaks-market.webp'} alt="Night view over San Francisco from Twin Peaks, with Market Street as a bright orange line of lights running toward the downtown towers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Market Street from Twin Peaks, 9:39 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Zoom in and downtown resolves into its landmarks. The tallest, with its crown of lights glowing at the top, is
    <strong>Salesforce Tower</strong>, finished in 2018 and the tallest building in the city. Around it are the
    lit windows of the Financial District, a few neon signs, and the red lights of towers.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'twin-peaks-downtown.webp'} alt="Zoomed view of downtown San Francisco at night, with the lit top of Salesforce Tower, office towers and the glowing line of Market Street." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown, zoomed in, 9:40 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then we turned around and looked up. A bright moon hung over the city, two or three days short of full, and
    near it was a single brilliant point of light, far too bright to be a star. Late November 2023 was when the
    moon passed close to Jupiter, so that is almost certainly what it was. A phone is not built for this, and the
    photos show it: the planet came out as a starburst, and the moon as an overexposed white disc. We took them
    anyway.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'twin-peaks-planet.webp'} alt="A single bright point of light with long diffraction spikes against a black night sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A bright planet, very likely Jupiter, 9:46 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'twin-peaks-moon.webp'} alt="A bright, nearly full moon in a black sky, with a faint point of light near it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The moon over Twin Peaks, 9:46 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Treasure Island in the morning</h3>
  <p>
    Saturday morning was the opposite of Friday night: cold, clear and very blue. At 11:18 am we were on the shore
    path of <strong>Treasure Island</strong>, in the middle of the bay. The island is artificial. It was built from
    dredged fill in the 1930s to host the 1939 Golden Gate International Exposition, then became a naval base for
    half a century. Today it is best known for one thing: the view.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ti-golden-gate.webp'} alt="A wide view across choppy blue bay water to the Golden Gate Bridge, Alcatraz and the Marin Headlands under a clear sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From Treasure Island: the Golden Gate, Alcatraz and the Marin Headlands, 11:18 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Looking west across the water, the whole mouth of the bay lines up in one frame. The Golden Gate Bridge stretches
    across the strait, the brown hills of the Marin Headlands rise behind it, and in front sits
    <strong>Alcatraz</strong>, the former federal prison, low and pale on its rock. With a long zoom, the
    cellhouse, the lighthouse and the water tower are all easy to pick out. It was the clearest air of any of our
    visits.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ti-alcatraz.webp'} alt="Alcatraz Island with its buildings and lighthouse in front of the Golden Gate Bridge and the hills of Marin." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Alcatraz, with the Golden Gate behind it.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ti-gate-and-island.webp'} alt="The Golden Gate Bridge spanning the strait, with Alcatraz at right, across blue water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bridge and the island, side by side.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Turn south and the view changes completely. The rocky shore of the island leads the eye across the water to the
    San Francisco skyline, with the <strong>Bay Bridge</strong> running off to the left. The bridge opened in 1936,
    six months before the Golden Gate, and it carries far more traffic. Its western span is a pair of suspension
    bridges joined in the middle, and from Treasure Island you see it nearly side-on, with the morning sun glittering
    on the water underneath.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ti-rocks.webp'} alt="Dark rocks in the foreground with the bay and the low San Francisco skyline in the distance under a bright sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The rocky shore, with the city in the distance.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ti-bay-bridge.webp'} alt="The western span of the Bay Bridge in silhouette over glittering water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Bay Bridge, backlit.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    And straight ahead is the skyline. From here downtown is one compact block of towers rising out of the water.
    Salesforce Tower stands in the middle, rounded at the top. To the right is the white spike of the
    <strong>Transamerica Pyramid</strong>, which was the city's signature building for over forty years. In the
    hard light of late morning the whole skyline was in silhouette. It is the classic postcard view, and on a day
    like that it is easy to see why.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ti-skyline.webp'} alt="The San Francisco skyline in silhouette across the bay, with Salesforce Tower in the middle and the Transamerica Pyramid at right." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The San Francisco skyline from Treasure Island, 11:19 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    After that the weekend turned social. There was a long lunch in Millbrae, which is a story that belongs to
    the people who were at the table, not to this page, and by evening we were down in Palo Alto, where dinner was
    Spanish. On Sunday, November 26, we stopped at the Computer History Museum in Mountain View, the same museum I
    had visited on the 2022 loop, and then drove home. Somewhere between Twin Peaks and Treasure
    Island, though, the Bay Area had stopped being a destination and become a habit.
  </p>

  <h2>Thanksgiving, again: November 2025</h2>
  <p>
    Two years later, Thanksgiving brought us north again. This time the Bay Area was the start and the end of
    something bigger: a five-day drive up to Crater Lake and Portland and back down the Oregon coast, finishing late
    at night in Napa. It is told in full in <a href="/misc/oregon-2025">Thanksgiving, the Long Way Round</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="jun-2026">A week in June 2026</h2>
  <p>
    In the summer of 2026 I spent a few months working in Palo Alto, so for once the Bay Area was where I lived
    rather than a place we visited. For one week, June 14 to 21, my wife came up to
    stay. She drove up from Southern California on her own, with Wangcai in the back seat. Her camera roll shows one
    stop on the way, at 5:27 pm in Kettleman City: a roadside shop with an old green pickup parked indoors under a
    shed roof, given a pair of cartoon eyes and a sign that reads "No Cussin'".
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kettleman-truck.webp'} alt="An old rusty green pickup truck parked inside a shop under a wooden shed roof, with cartoon eyes on its windshield and a hand-painted sign reading No Cussin'." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A stop in Kettleman City on the drive up, June 14, 5:27 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On the weekdays the two of them explored on their own. Monday began at a coffee shop near the
    Palo Alto Baylands, where Wangcai spent a long time staring out through the glass door. At 12:20 my wife was
    looking out over the Baylands themselves: dry grass, a gravel path, channels of water winding through the
    marsh, and the hills of the East Bay on the far side. On Tuesday morning the coffee was at a Starbucks, and
    Wangcai sat for her portrait in a red-checked bandana.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'baylands-2026.webp'} alt="Dry grass sloping down to a gravel path and winding channels of water in a green marsh, with brown hills across the bay under a clear sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Palo Alto Baylands, June 15, 12:20 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-cafe-2026.webp'} alt="A white dog in a red-checked bandana sitting on a tiled café floor beside a table with a purple iced drink." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai at morning coffee, June 16.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    On Thursday evening, June 18, we drove up to San Francisco together for dinner. At 6:48 pm we were on the
    freeway coming into the city, with Salesforce Tower straight ahead under a sky full of big white clouds.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sf-approach-2026.webp'} alt="The San Francisco skyline with Salesforce Tower at its center, seen from an elevated freeway under large white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Coming into the city, June 18, 6:48 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Dinner was at a restaurant on the Embarcadero, at a table by the window. Across the road, past a row of palm
    trees, the Bay Bridge ran out toward the East Bay. We started with oysters, and later came sea urchin on toast,
    scallops in their shells and a whole grilled fish. The meal ran from about seven until after nine, and by the
    end the sky over the bridge had gone dark blue.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'embarcadero-window.webp'} alt="A restaurant window looking out past palm trees and streetlights to the Bay Bridge under a cloudy evening sky, with a tray of oysters on ice on the table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Bay Bridge from our table, 7:42 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'embarcadero-uni.webp'} alt="Two pieces of toast topped with orange sea urchin on a cut-glass plate." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sea urchin on toast, 8:21 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    On Saturday evening we went out for dinner at Zareen's, and on Sunday, June 21, my wife drove back south.
  </p>

  <h2 id="aug-2026">The city, then Santa Rosa: August 2026</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-2026-08.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-2026-08.webp'} alt="Map of August 15 and 16, 2026: San Francisco on Saturday, then Petaluma, Santa Rosa, Napa and Berkeley on Sunday." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>San Francisco on Saturday; Petaluma, Santa Rosa, Napa and Berkeley on Sunday.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Two months later we spent a weekend in the city and north of it: San Francisco on Saturday, August 15,
    then Santa Rosa on Sunday, back by way of Napa and Berkeley. Wangcai came too. Saturday started with coffee at
    The Coffee Movement on Washington Street, at an outdoor counter looking down the hill: a tray of three small
    glasses and an iced coffee.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'coffee-washington.webp'} alt="A tray of three small glasses of dark coffee and an iced coffee in a plastic cup on an outdoor counter, with a city street, apartment buildings and a spire beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Coffee on Washington Street, August 15, 12:28 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Lunch was a few blocks away in Chinatown, at a Jiangxi restaurant. The soups came in brown clay crocks stamped
    南昌瓦罐, Nanchang crock soup, one with meat and one with seaweed. After them came a claypot braise, a stir-fry
    and a bowl of noodles in a milky broth.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nanchang-crocks.webp'} alt="Two brown clay soup crocks on saucers, one stamped with the Chinese characters for Nanchang crock soup, with a cup of tea behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Nanchang crock soup in Chinatown, 12:45 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    In the afternoon we had ice cream at Philmore Creamery on Fillmore Street, which has a sign under its front window:
    "Dog parking only. All other animals will be towed." Then we drove north over the hill. Where Fillmore Street
    crosses the crest of Pacific Heights, the road drops away so steeply that the whole street below, the bay and
    the hills on the far side line up in one view.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'philmore-dog-parking.webp'} alt="A red ledge under a shop window painted with the words Dog Parking Only, all other animals will be towed, below a Philmore Creamery sign." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Philmore Creamery, 3:06 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fillmore-hill.webp'} alt="A very steep city street dropping down between houses and trees toward the blue bay, with hills across the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fillmore Street from the top of the hill, 3:26 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Dinner was Peruvian, at La Mar on the Embarcadero, a short walk up the waterfront from where we had eaten in June. There were
    pisco sours, two ceviches, a causa of purple potato topped with crab, and later grilled octopus. When we left at
    7:40, the fog had come in over downtown, and the top of Salesforce Tower had disappeared into it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'la-mar-ceviche.webp'} alt="Three bowls on a dark table: a purple potato causa topped with crab and roe, a ceviche in a yellow sauce, and a ceviche with corn kernels and red onion, beside a foamy pisco sour." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Ceviches and a causa at La Mar, 6:37 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'salesforce-fog.webp'} alt="Salesforce Tower with its top hidden in fog, next to another tall office tower, with trees and palms below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Salesforce Tower in the fog, 7:40 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    On Sunday we drove north. We were in downtown Petaluma just before noon and in Santa Rosa by one. Santa Rosa was
    Charles Schulz's home. Outside the old stone
    railroad depot in Railroad Square, Lucy stood with her arms wide open in a pink dress, next to Woodstock
    perched on top of his nest.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'santa-rosa-peanuts.webp'} alt="Statues of Lucy in a pink dress with her arms open and of Woodstock sitting on a nest, outside a stone building with a Gifts and Souvenirs window." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lucy and Woodstock in Railroad Square, Santa Rosa, August 16, 1:01 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From Santa Rosa we crossed over to Napa for a hot dog at three, and then drove south into the East Bay. In
    Berkeley, at 6:35, dessert came to the table smoking: a tall bowl of cream and chocolate on a metal stand, with
    dry-ice fog pouring over its sides, and a slice of crêpe cake next to it. We were back in the South Bay that
    night.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'berkeley-dessert.webp'} alt="A metal bowl on a stand piled with whipped cream and chocolate pieces, with white dry-ice fog spilling over its sides." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dessert in Berkeley, 6:35 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Santa Cruz and Carmel: August 2026</h2>
  <p>
    At the end of the summer, a trip to Sacramento and Lake Tahoe ended by crossing to the coast for
    Santa Cruz and Carmel-by-the-Sea, a few miles from where we had watched the seals on 17-Mile Drive. That story is
    in <a href="/misc/tahoe-2026">Two Capitols and a Very Clear Lake</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Why we keep coming back</h2>
  <p>
    Put the seven visits side by side and a pattern appears. We have now arrived by plane at dusk and by car at night.
    We have seen the Golden Gate from the Presidio cliffs and from the middle of the bay, and the whole city from
    Twin Peaks, once in the early evening in 2022 and again at night. We have been to the same castle twice, in May and in November, walked the same Capitola street a year
    apart, and eaten on the Embarcadero twice in one summer. None of these trips was planned as a return visit. They just kept happening, because the Bay Area is on
    the way to everything we like: wine country to the north, the coast to the south, Oregon beyond.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is still a long list of things we haven't done. We haven't seen the Lone Cypress properly, crossed to
    Alcatraz, or spent an unhurried day in Napa without a long drive at either end. That is fine. At this rate we
    will be back soon.
  </p>
